    "created_at": "2022-09-27T09:24:20Z",
    "updated_at": "2022-09-27T09:24:20Z",
    "body": "I think the implication is that all audio controls are expected by browser developers to be horizontal by default, regardless of whether the accompanying text is set vertically or horizontally – which contradicts the opinion expressed by the chair of the Chinese Layout Requirements group.  It certainly does seem to cause a problem for inlined controls (as can be seen in the examples pointed to.)\r\n\r\nIt may be useful to also surface the other idea relative to vertical text raised in that discussion: ie. whether a vertical arrangement would contain 'upright' controls, or would run down the line (such as you'd get with `writing-mode:sideways-xx`).\r\n\r\n(The other question raised – and i don't know whether you want to address it here – relates to whether the progression of controls is affected by the direction of horizontal text when the reading direction is right-to-left.  This relates not only to the order of the controls, but also to whether or not any icons should be reversed if they represent controls that have a directional bias built in.  I have heard, in the past, people say that icons like ⏩ are not flipped.  I don't know whether that's because the inertia in changing them has made people get used to the LTR direction for these controls, or whether it's related to the fact that hardware buttons remain the same. [ALReq](https://www.w3.org/TR/alreq) doesn't offer any advice here.)",
